  "blocks": [
    {
      "box": [
        49.39310073852539,
        24.205934524536133,
        77.48339080810547,
        32.39178466796875
      ],
      "text": "Test 1\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        48.738887786865234,
        58.22492980957031,
        477.5497741699219,
        251.22901916503906
      ],
      "text": "E\nThe burial chamber of the tomb, where the king’s body was laid to rest, was dug\nbeneath the base of the pyramid, surrounded by a vast maze of long tunnels that\nhad rooms offthem to discourage robbers. One of the most mysterious discoveries\nfound inside the pyramid was a large number of stone vessels. Over 40,000 of\nthese vessels, of various forms and shapes, were discovered in storerooms off the\npyramid’s underground passages. They are inscribed with the names of rulers from\nthe First and Second Dynasties of Egypt and made from different kinds of stone.\nThere is no agreement among scholars and archaeologists on why the vessels\nwere placed in the tomb of Djoser or what they were supposed to represent. The\narchaeologist Jean-Philippe Lauer, who excavated most of the pyramid and complex,\nbelieves they were originally stored and then given a ‘proper burial’ by Djoser in his\npyramid to honor his predecessors. There are other historians, however, who claim\nthe vessels were dumped into the shafts as yet another attempt to prevent grave\nrobbers from getting to the king’s burial chamber.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        48.7388916015625,
        260.7039794921875,
        478.8268127441406,
        341.8375244140625
      ],
      "text": "F\nUnfortunately, all of the precautions and intricate design of the underground network\ndid not prevent ancient robbers from finding a way in. Djoser’s grave goods, and\neven his body, were stolen at some point in the past and all archaeologists found\nwere a small number of his valuables overlooked by the thieves. There was enough\nleft throughout the pyramid and its complex, however, to astonish and amaze the\narchaeologists who excavated it.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        48.411773681640625,
        351.3125,
        443.5157470703125,
        362.772705078125
      ],
      "text": "G___Egyptologist Miroslav Verner writes, ‘Few monuments hold a place in human\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        71.96345520019531,
        365.7052001953125,
        464.0105895996094,
        432.7731628417969
      ],
      "text": "history as significant as that of the Step Pyramid in Saqqara ... It can be said\nwithout exaggeration that this pyramid complex constitutes a milestone in the\nevolution of monumental stone architecture in Egypt and in the world as a whole.’\nThe Step Pyramid was a revolutionary advance in architecture and became the\narchetype which all the other great pyramid builders of Egypt would follow.\n"
    },
